This repository is here to hold the files I make while working through the Great Courses "How to Program Python 3."

The files are named to reflect their contents. As the course is presented in lectures, both in video and PDF (as a book format),
the files that contain the bulk of the code created while working through each lecture, those files are named in the following format:
lecture_01.py
where lecture indicates the content is from the lecture,
01 indicates the chapter of the content
py indicates that it is a Python file.

Other things might also be added to a file name:
lecture_01_02.py
In this example, the 02 indicates that this is the second Python file from that chapter. I might do this because the file has become
a little long to view or it also might be that the program that runs might be a little long to go through and I do not want to run
the whole thing time after time when it is really the new concepts I want to test while learning about the code.

Sometimes I might include other words in the file name:
lecture_01_03_input_prompts.py
input_prompts would indicate that the file contains concepts about learning how to use input prompts. I might also go beyond that
speciifc concept in the exploration on how to use that concept.

Other files that might be seen in this repository are CSV files that have the .csv file extension. That stands for
comma separated values
Text files with the .txt file extension might be seen.
Some files that are not Python files might have short names that are not really indicative as to what the content is. For information
on those files, they might be opened to have a look at the content. In general, the name of those files is probably documented
in a program file. The names might be shortened so I do not have to type a name that is long, but indicative of the content, everytime
I have to type it into the input prompt in the program.
